t=t.replace(' This batch-1 supplier is present as an authored draft and its decision/use remain open.','')
t=fact(t,13,r'''[F13] $C_c(G)$ is dense in $L^1(G)$ and $L^2(G)$ ([[def-complex-haar-lp-spaces-and-compactly-supported-functions]], [[lem-complex-haar-l1-and-l2-are-complete-and-cc-dense]]). The Iwasawa coordinates are smooth and give a positive smooth Haar density ([[thm-iwasawa-decomposition-for-sl2-r]]); the local coordinate argument in step 1.4 proves the required smooth density.''')
newfacts=r'''[F14] Measurable Gram–Schmidt gives frames, measurable closed subfields and density of bounded finite-measure scalar localizations ([[lem-measurable-gram-schmidt-and-constant-field-trivializations]]). Countably many primitive ideal-opens generate the standard primitive-code Borel structure, with hull-kernel norm superlevels open ([[lem-primitive-ideals-have-standard-borel-quotient-norm-codings]], [[def-primitive-ideal-space-of-a-group-c-star-algebra]]). Borel class maps and conull inverse selections use [[lem-local-analytic-separation-and-saturated-borel-quotients]], [[lem-closed-witness-codings-and-measured-projections]], [[lem-borel-relations-admit-conull-borel-uniformizations]]. Operators commuting with the scalar diagonal are decomposable and their fibre representatives are unique almost everywhere ([[thm-decomposable-operators-are-the-commutant-of-diagonal-multiplication]], [[thm-measurable-essentially-bounded-operator-fields-act-decomposably]]); these direct integrals are Hilbert spaces ([[thm-direct-integrals-of-measurable-hilbert-fields-are-hilbert-spaces]]).

[F15] Hilbert–Schmidt norm is the square-sum of matrix entries and is basis independent; left/right multiplication is bounded by the corresponding operator norms ([[thm-hilbert-schmidt-norm-is-basis-independent]], [[thm-hilbert-schmidt-operators-form-a-two-sided-ideal]]). Complex $L^2$ is Hilbert, including for counting measure ([[lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space]], [[def-counting-measure]], [[prop-counting-measure-is-a-measure]]).

[F16] Smooth compactly supported Euclidean mollifiers are approximate identities, and convolution with them is smooth with derivatives obtained by differentiating the kernel ([[prop-mollifier-families-are-l-one-approximate-identities]], [[thm-convolution-with-a-mollifier-is-smooth-and-differentiates-under-the-integral-sign]]). A nonnegative function of integral zero vanishes almost everywhere ([[thm-nonnegative-integral-zero-iff-zero-almost-everywhere]]).

'''
t=t.replace('[A1] AC supplies',newfacts+'[A1] AC supplies',1)
a=t.index('## Open obligations\n');b=t.index('## Proof\n',a);t=t[:a]+t[b:]
# Make positivity/smoothing domains explicit in the existing local Hardy witness.
t=t.replace('On smooth vectors, $\\lambda(D)=',r'''For any strongly continuous unitary representation, integration against a smooth compact kernel gives smooth vectors: differentiating $\pi(\exp(sX))\pi(\phi)$ differentiates the translated kernel in $L^1$, so every iterated derivative is the bounded integrated derivative of that kernel. On such vectors, $\lambda(D)=''')
t=t.replace('On $I_{0,\\nu}$, [F5] gives',r'''The Casimir scalar of [F5] holds on these smooth vectors as well: it commutes with every $K$-projection, each projected vector is a smooth finite $K$-type, and the dense $K$-decomposition forces the difference from the claimed scalar to vanish. On $I_{0,\nu}$, [F5] gives''')
# Add exact smooth approximation before constructing the Hilbert isometry.
a=t.index('\n2.1 Dividing');t=t[:a]+r'''
1.4 In the global smooth coordinates $K\times\mathbb R^2$ of [F2], write a compactly supported continuous function as $F(\theta,t,x)$, periodic in $\theta$. Periodize a nonnegative compact smooth Euclidean mollifier in the first coordinate and convolve in all three coordinates. The resulting functions are smooth and have support in one slightly enlarged compact cylinder. Uniform continuity makes them converge uniformly to $F$: the error is bounded by the supremum of $|F(z-h)-F(z)|$ for small shifts $h$. The smooth positive Haar weight $e^t/(2\pi)$ is bounded on that cylinder, whose Haar mass is finite, so convergence holds in both $L^1$ and $L^2$. Together with [F13], this proves $C_c^\infty(G)$ dense in both spaces. For each member of the countable $C_c$-dense family of [F10], choose one such smooth approximant within $1/k$ in $L^1$ for every positive integer $k$; enumerating the pairs gives a countable smooth $L^1$-dense family $(u_j)$. The same local bumps, normalized by their positive Haar integral, supply the smooth approximate identities used in step 1.3 and step 6.1. [F2, F10, F13, F16, A1, algebra]
'''+t[a:]
a=t.index('\n3.1 In the source');t=t[:a]+r'''
2.2 Let $X$ be the disjoint union of two copies of $(0,\infty)$, labelled $(\varepsilon,s)$, and the countable discrete labels $(n,+),(n,-)$ for $n\ge2$. Use the explicit compact-picture representation $\sigma_x=I_{\varepsilon,is}$ or the indicated discrete model. The field has fixed countable orthonormal bases on each component. The principal coefficients are Borel in $s$ by the compact-picture continuity of [F7], with off-diagonal coefficients obtained by polarization; discrete components are countable. Thus [F14] gives a Borel class map $q:X\to\widehat G$, and [F3] makes it injective. Give $X$ the native positive-parameter densities $s\tanh(\pi s/2)/(8\pi)$ and $s\coth(\pi s/2)/(8\pi)$, and masses $(n-1)/(4\pi)$ at each discrete label. Call this sigma-finite measure $w$. The doubling of the continuous densities follows from the sign equivalence of [F3]: the two redundant signs have equal Hilbert–Schmidt norms by unitary conjugation and equal density. Put $\mu=q_*w$. This measure is on the actual dual, with no separate fibre for each redundant sign. [F3, F6, F7, F14, F15, step 2.1, A1, construct]

2.3 The class transfer requires no global representative selector. Choose an equivalent probability $P$ on $X$ by positive summable weights on a finite-measure exhaustion. Its pushforward $\beta=q_*P$ is equivalent to $\mu$. The Borel graph relation $\{(b,x):q(x)=b\}$ has completion-measurable image by [F14], and that image has full $\beta$-measure. Choose a conull Borel $B\subseteq q(X)$ and apply the conull selection in [F14] to obtain a Borel $x(b)$ with $q(x(b))=b$ there. Injectivity makes this the inverse of $q$ on $q^{-1}B$. The sets $\{b\in B:x(b)\in E_j\}$ for a finite-$w$-measure exhaustion of $X$ show that $\mu$ is sigma-finite. Transport the representation and its basis to $B$, extending the fibre by zero off $B$. A matrix $(t_{ij})$ with square-summable entries defines a bounded operator: for a finite vector $v$, Cauchy–Schwarz gives $\|Tv\|^2\le\sum_{ij}|t_{ij}|^2\|v\|^2$. It extends to the whole carrier, and [F15] identifies its Hilbert–Schmidt norm with this square-sum. Hence $\mathrm{HS}(H_b)$ is the Hilbert space $\ell^2$ of the matrix entries, by [F15], with rank-one matrix units as an orthonormal basis. These units give a countable measurable fundamental family for the Hilbert–Schmidt field. Its direct integral is Hilbert by [F14]. The entries of $\sigma_b(f)$ are Borel for each smooth test $f$ by the integrated Borel correspondence, and their square-sum is measurable. No assertion that every test is Hilbert–Schmidt at every dual point is needed; the norm identity will supply almost-everywhere finiteness. [F3, F14, F15, step 2.2, A1, construct]
'''+t[a:]
t=step(t,'4.1',r'''4.1 Rescale the norm identity in step 1.2 using $dg=2dg_H$, $\mu=\frac12\mu_H$ in the redundant parameter and $\sigma(f)=2\sigma_H(f)$, then use the sign identification in step 2.2. It gives $\|f\|_2^2=\int\|\sigma_b(f)\|_{\rm HS}^2d\mu(b)$ on $C_c^\infty(G)$. Thus the matrix field of step 2.3 is Hilbert–Schmidt almost everywhere and defines an isometry $\mathcal F$. Step 1.4 and Hilbert completeness extend it uniquely to an isometry $L^2(G,dg)\to\mathcal H_\mu:=\int^\oplus\mathrm{HS}(H_b)d\mu(b)$ with closed range. Left and right translations $f(x)\mapsto f(g^{-1}x)$ and $f(x)\mapsto f(xg)$ give $\mathcal F(\lambda(g)f)_b=\sigma_b(g)\mathcal F(f)_b$ and $\mathcal F(\rho(g)f)_b=\mathcal F(f)_b\sigma_b(g)^{-1}$ by Haar change of variables. Both target actions are unitary by [F15] and strongly continuous: on a rank-one matrix this follows from strong continuity of $\sigma_b$, finite matrix truncation gives fibre continuity, and the bound $2\|T_b\|_{\rm HS}$ gives continuity of the integrated action by truncation of its integrable squared norm. Density extends the intertwining identities to all $L^2$. [F2, F12, F13, F14, F15, step 1.2, step 1.4, step 2.2, step 2.3, step 3.1, A1, algebra]''')
t=step(t,'5.2',r'''5.2 Put $A=C^*(G)$ and let $L$ denote the left action on $\mathcal H_\mu$, so $L(a)_bT=\sigma_b(a)T$. For any closed ideal $J$ of $A$, its intrinsic support projection $P_J$ onto $\overline{L(J)\mathcal H_\mu}$ commutes with $L(A)$ and its commutant: the ideal span reduces $L(A)$, and every commutant operator and its adjoint preserve that span. Therefore $P_J\in L(A)''$. It is precisely the scalar multiplier of the ideal-open $\{b:J\not\subseteq\ker\sigma_b\}$. To verify this, take a countable dense sequence in $J$, apply its represented operators to the countable matrix fundamental family, and use [F14] for the measurable closed spans. In an irreducible fibre a nonzero ideal has full support; its approximate unit tends strongly to1 on $H_b$, hence left multiplication tends to1 on Hilbert–Schmidt matrices by finite-column truncation and the uniform norm bound. The fibre ideal span is therefore all of $\mathrm{HS}(H_b)$ or0. Its global span equals the integral of those spans: bounded finite-measure scalar localizations of the generating sections are $L(j)$ of localized fundamental sections and lie in the global span, and conversely every $L(j)\xi$ takes values in the fibre spans; their density is [F14]. Countably many such ideal-opens generate all dual Borel sets by [F3,F14]. Their indicator multipliers generate the full scalar diagonal algebra by monotone strong limits of indicators and bounded simple approximation. This is the actual ideal-support argument of [F4] applied to the present field. If $Q$ is the closed-range projection of step 4.1, the left/right intertwining and inverse group actions make its range reducing, so $Q$ commutes with both target actions. In particular it commutes with $L(A)''$ and the intrinsic scalar diagonal. By [F14] it is decomposable, $Q=\int^\oplus Q_b\,d\mu(b)$. [F3, F4, F14, F15, step 2.3, step 4.1, A1, algebra]''')
a=t.index('\n6.1 If $Q');t=t[:a]+r'''
5.3 On a single conull set, the projection field $Q_b$ commutes with both target group actions: apply uniqueness of decomposable fields [F14] to each commutator at a fixed countable dense subset of $G$, remove the countable union of null exceptions, and extend by the fibre strong continuity proved in step 4.1. Here is the joint irreducibility calculation. On the Hilbert–Schmidt matrix space, view each column as a copy of $H_b$. Each column block of a bounded operator commuting with left $\sigma_b(G)$ is a bounded self-intertwiner of $\sigma_b$, hence scalar by [F9]. The scalar column matrix defines a bounded operator $C$ on the column $\ell^2$ (test finite columns with one fixed unit row vector); thus the original operator is $I\otimes C$. Right multiplication by $\sigma_b(g)^{-1}$ acts on these columns by the conjugate representation. That representation is irreducible, because conjugating a closed invariant subspace gives one for $\sigma_b$. A second use of [F9] makes $C$ scalar. Applied to the projection $Q_b$, this gives $Q_b=0$ or $I$ almost everywhere. [F9, F14, F15, step 4.1, step 5.2, algebra]
'''+t[a:]
t=step(t,'6.1',r'''6.1 Suppose the measurable zero-fibre set $E=\{b:Q_b=0\}$ has positive measure; measurability follows from its countable matrix coefficients. For every smooth member $u_j$ of the countable $L^1$-dense family in step 1.4, its transform lies in the range of $Q$, so $\sigma_b(u_j)=0$ almost everywhere on $E$. Remove the countable union of these exceptions and choose $b\in E$ where its irreducible carrier is nonzero. Contractivity $\|\sigma_b(f)-\sigma_b(u_j)\|\le\|f-u_j\|_1$ gives $\sigma_b(f)=0$ for every $f\in L^1(G)$. But for a nonzero vector $v$, a normalized smooth bump sufficiently near $e$ has $\|\sigma_b(\eta)v-v\|<\|v\|/2$ by strong continuity, contradicting this vanishing. Thus $Q_b=I$ almost everywhere and $\mathcal F$ is onto. Left multiplication on the Hilbert–Schmidt field is the amplification of $\sigma_b$ with multiplicity $\dim H_b$, as seen by its columns. It is consequently an actual irreducible disintegration of the regular representation; the canonical central/refinement and uniqueness interfaces of [F4] apply to this constructed model. [F4, F10, F14, F15, step 1.4, step 5.3, A1, algebra]''')
a=t.index('\n7.1 The carrier');t=t[:a]+r'''
6.2 Let $S$ be the closed support of $\mu$. The dual has a countable base by [F3,F14], so the complement of $S$ is a countable union of measure-zero basic opens; thus $\mu$ is carried by $S$. For $a\in A$, onto disintegration gives $a\in\ker\lambda$ exactly when $\sigma_b(a)=0$ almost everywhere, by uniqueness of decomposable fields. The quotient norm superlevel $\{b:\|\sigma_b(a)\|>r\}$ is open by [F3,F14]. If it met $S$ for $r>0$, it would have positive measure, contradicting this almost-everywhere vanishing. Conversely vanishing at every point of $S$ implies almost-everywhere vanishing. Hence $\ker\lambda=\bigcap_{b\in S}\ker\sigma_b$. By [F8], an irreducible is weakly contained in $\lambda$ exactly when it is in the Fell closure of $S$, which is $S$ itself. This proves the support/tempered bridge locally for the constructed measure. [F3, F8, F14, F16, step 6.1, algebra]
'''+t[a:]
t=step(t,'7.1',r'''7.1 The measure in steps 2.2–3.1 is carried by the stated principal/discrete classes; step 5.1 puts every such class and precisely the stated endpoint candidates in its closed support. Step 1.3 excludes every positive-parameter spherical complementary class and the trivial class from that support by the positive reduced-algebra witness and step 6.2, rather than by zero mass. Classification [F3] leaves no further classes. The endpoint fibres have no atoms, and the odd zero direct sum is not a dual point. Steps 6.1–6.2 establish the full regular disintegration, onto transform, canonical measure-class/multiplicity and exact weak-containment support. The Haar conversion and every field, range, endpoint and exclusion argument are local; the original trace identity [F1] is the only cited exception. [F3, F4, F6, F7, F8, step 1.3, step 2.2, step 3.1, step 5.1, step 6.1, step 6.2] ∎''')
p.write_text(t)
